  "query_preview": "..(Friday through Wednesday)\nIssued at 528 PM EDT WED SEP 9 2026\n\nGeneral model agreement remains regarding the long wave pattern\nthrough the middle of next week. Amplified and progressive flow\nwill modulate across the northern half of the CONUS, while ridging\ngradually consolidates and expands west over the southeastern\nportion of the U.S. A positively tilted trough will be aligned \nfrom New England through the Ohio Valley and into the southern \nPlains beginning on Friday. At the surface, a weak cold front \nwill be positioned near the Tennessee border back towards the \nlower Mississippi Valley. Eastern Kentucky will find itself in an\nunsettled pattern continuing into early this weekend, as\ntroughing is maintained aloft, and the surface boundary lingers in\nthe vicinity of the region. Model guidance has favored a slower \ntransition to increasing 500 mb heights compared to yesterday. \nThis will result in higher PoPs across the area through Saturday,\nwith some locally heavy rainers still possible.\n\nSunday through Tuesday, more decisive ridging will build in \naloft, which will limit the areal coverage of any convection. \nUnfortunately, this will also allow for more above normal heat and\nhumidity, with highs returning to the mid to upper 80s, and even a\nsome lower 90s at times. Dew points will also likely exceed 70 \ndegrees for the majority of the period. By Wednesday, the upper\nlevel ridge may broaden and pivot enough to allow for enough \npassing short wave energy to drive the next surface cold front \nsouth of the Ohio River. This will bring another uptick in PoPs \nby Wednesday, although confidence in timing is pretty low this far\nout.",
